{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nvoid strcpy(char *s, char *t) { while(*s++ = *t++); } // this fixes the issue\nNow the string will be properly null-terminated, and the output will be exactly: Hello world! (option d)\n5. ANSWER:25\n1. Variable Initialization:\n\u2022 a = 20\n\u2022 b = 25\n\u2022 z is a pointer to an integer, which initially does not point to any variable.\n2. Pointer Assignment:\n\u2022 z = &a;\nThe pointer z is now pointing to the address of variable a.\n3. Function Call:\n\u2022 The function foo(z, b) is called. Here, z points to a, and b is passed as the second argument (which is 25).\n\u2022 Inside the function foo(int *p, int x), *p = x; assigns the value of x (which is 25) to the location pointed to by p.\n\u2022 Since p is pointing to a, a is updated to 25.\n4. Printing the Value of a:\n\u2022 After the function call, a has been updated to 25.\n\u2022 The printf(\"%d\", a); statement prints the value of a, which is now 25."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Programming in C\",\n  \"topic\": \"Runtime Environment\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"C String Functions (strcpy)\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q112\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"void strcpy(char *s, char *t) { while(*s++ = *t++); } // this fixes the issue\\nNow the string will be properly null-terminated, and the output will be exactly: Hello world! (option d)\\n5. ANSWER:25\\n1. Variable Initialization:\\n\\u2022 a = 20\\n\\u2022 b = 25\\n\\u2022 z is a pointer to an integer, which initially does not point to any variable.\\n2. Pointer Assignment:\\n\\u2022 z = &a;\\nThe pointer z is now pointing to the address of variable a.\\n3. Function Call:\\n\\u2022 The function foo(z, b) is called. Here, z points to a, and b is passed as the second argument (which is 25).\\n\\u2022 Inside the function foo(int *p, int x), *p = x; assigns the value of x (which is 25) to the location pointed to by p.\\n\\u2022 Since p is pointing to a, a is updated to 25.\\n4. Printing the Value of a:\\n\\u2022 After the function call, a has been updated to 25.\\n\\u2022 The printf(\\\"%d\\\", a); statement prints the value of a, which is now 25.\",\n  \"options\": [],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"\\\\0: Null character\\nPointer Arithmetic (C/C++)\\nptr + n: Address of ptr + n * sizeof(*ptr)\\nptr - n: Address of ptr - n * sizeof(*ptr)\\nptr1 - ptr2: (address_of_ptr1 - address_of_ptr2) / sizeof(*ptr1)\\nC String Functions (from <string.h>)\\nstrlen(s): Length of string s (excluding '\\\\0').\\nstrcpy(dest, src): Copies src to dest.\\nstrcat(dest, src): Appends src to dest.\\nstrcmp(s1, s2): Compares s1 and s2. Returns 0 if equal.\\nStorage Class Properties (C/C++)\",\n    \"5.15\\n5.15.1\\nA. gnirts\\nB. string\\nC. gnirt\\nD. no output is printed\\n5.15.2\\nA. @#Hello World!\\nB. Hello World!\\nC. ello World!\\nD. Hello World!d!\\n5.16\\n5.16.1\\nA. A static variable declared inside a function\\nB. An array of integers declared inside a function\\nC. A dynamically allocated array of integers created using malloc() function call\\nD. Return address of a function\\ngatecse-2026-set2 programming-in-c runtime-environment one-mark\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nStrings (2)\\nStrings: GATE CSE 2004 | Question: 33\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
