{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nElements sequentially:\n\u25cf 1st insertion: 0 steps\n\u25cf 2nd insertion: 1 step\n\u25cf 3rd insertion: 2 steps\n\u25cf nth insertion: n \u2212 1 steps\nMathematical Sum: The total number of operations is the sum of the first n \u2212 1 integers:\n1. next = curr.next (Store the next node)\n2. curr.next = prev (Reverse the link)\n3. prev = curr (Advance the prev pointer)\nn\u22121\n\u2211 i = i=1\n(n \u2212 1)n\n2\n4. curr = next (Advance the curr pointer)\nComplexity Analysis:\n\uf0b7 Space Complexity: The algorithm allocates memory only for three node pointers. This memory requirement does not scale with the input size n. Therefore, the space complexity is strictly O(1).\n\uf0b7 Time Complexity: The algorithm traverses the linked list exactly once. For a linked list containing n nodes, the loop executes exactly n times. Since the operations inside the loop take constant time, the total time taken is directly proportional to n. Thus, the time complexity is tightly bound at \ud835\udf03(\ud835\udc5b) for all cases, including the worst case.\nConclusion: This results in a quadratic growth rate, expressed as \ud835\udee9(\ud835\udc5b2).\n7. Answer: B\nExplanation: For insertion of node at the beginning of linked list only need manipulation of pointers so \u03b8(1) time. But if we have pointer to the tail of the list in order to delete it, we need the address of the 2nd last node which can be obtained by traversing the list which takes O(n) time.\n8. Answer: (B)\nExplanation: Function join(m, n) works as follows: It starts with p = n Then traverses list n using while (p->next != NULL)\n9. Answer:C\nExplanation: We analyze each operation on a sorted doubly linked list. Delete: Given pointer to node, deletion in DLL takes O(1). Done 