{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nFor height to be 6, at every step after choosing the root, all remaining elements must go only to one side (either all smaller or all greater). First, choose the root. If root is 1, then all remaining 6 elements (2\u20137) must go to the right side. If root is 7, then all remaining 6 elements (1\u20136) must go to the left side. If root is anything between 2 and 6, then some elements will go left and some right, so height will be less than 6. So only 1 or 7 can be the root. After fixing the root (say 1), the remaining 6 elements must again form a skewed BST of height 5. So the problem reduces recursively. For 7 nodes to form a chain, at each step we must choose either the smallest or the largest element among the remaining elements. At first step: 2 choices (1 or 7). At second step: again 2 choices (smallest or largest of remaining 6). At third step: again 2 choices. This continues for 6 steps. Total ways = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 = 2\u2076 = 64"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Data Structures\",\n  \"topic\": \"Binary Search Trees\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Binary Search Trees can be constructed in such a way that they form a skewed structure (either left-heavy or right-heavy) when the height is fixed.\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": true,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q151\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"For height to be 6, at every step after choosing the root, all remaining elements must go only to one side (either all smaller or all greater). First, choose the root. If root is 1, then all remaining 6 elements (2\\u20137) must go to the right side. If root is 7, then all remaining 6 elements (1\\u20136) must go to the left side. If root is anything between 2 and 6, then some elements will go left and some right, so height will be less than 6. So only 1 or 7 can be the root. After fixing the root (say 1), the remaining 6 elements must again form a skewed BST of height 5. So the problem reduces recursively. For 7 nodes to form a chain, at each step we must choose either the smallest or the largest element among the remaining elements. At first step: 2 choices (1 or 7). At second step: again 2 choices (smallest or largest of remaining 6). At third step: again 2 choices. This continues for 6 steps. Total ways = 2 \\u00d7 2 \\u00d7 2 \\u00d7 2 \\u00d7 2 \\u00d7 2 = 2\\u2076 = 64\",\n  \"options\": [],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"Typically implemented using an array.\\nFormulas/Theorems:\\nParent of node : \\n (for 0-indexed array).\\nLeft child of node : \\n (for 0-indexed array).\\nRight child of node : \\n (for 0-indexed array).\\nHeight of a heap with \\n nodes: \\n.\\nProperties/Identities:\\nMin-Heap: Root is the minimum element.\\nMax-Heap: Root is the maximum element.\\nComplete Binary Tree: All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level, which is filled from left to\\nright.\\nOperations Time Complexity:\\nInsert:\",\n    \"inserted as a child of the node \\u201cg\\u201d?\\ngate1996 data-structures binary-tree avl-tree normal\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nAVL Tree: GATE CSE 1998 | Question: 21\\nA. Derive a recurrence relation for the size of the smallest AVL tree with height .\\nB. What is the size of the smallest AVL tree with height ?\\ngate1998 data-structures avl-tree descriptive numerical-answers\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nAVL Tree: GATE CSE 2009 | Question: 37,ISRO-DEC2017-55\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
