{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nRipple carry adder (RCA) time complexity is \\(\\Theta(n)\\). Carry Look Ahead Adder (CLA) computes carries in parallel using Generate and Propagate signals. For each bit i: Generate: \\(G_i = A_i B_i\\) Propagate: \\(P_i = A_i \\oplus B_i\\) Carry equation: \\(C_{i+1} = G_i + P_i C_i\\) Using expansion: \\(C_1 = G_0 + P_0 C_0\\) \\(C_2 = G_1 + P_1 G_0 + P_1 P_0 C_0\\) \\(C_3 = G_2 + P_2 G_1 + P_2 P_1 G_0 + P_2 P_1 P_0 C_0\\) ... \\(C_n\\) can be expressed directly using logic expressions. Fan-in restriction: The question states gates have fan-in \u2264 2. So large AND/OR operations cannot be done in one step. They must be built as a tree of 2-input gates. A tree structure computing n inputs has depth: Depth = \\(\\log_2(n)\\) Therefore, Total addition time \u221d depth of carry computation tree = \\(\\Theta(\\log n)\\)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Algorithms\",\n  \"topic\": \"Time Complexity\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Time complexity of carry look-ahead adder (CLA)\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": true,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q231\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"Ripple carry adder (RCA) time complexity is \\\\(\\\\Theta(n)\\\\). Carry Look Ahead Adder (CLA) computes carries in parallel using Generate and Propagate signals. For each bit i: Generate: \\\\(G_i = A_i B_i\\\\) Propagate: \\\\(P_i = A_i \\\\oplus B_i\\\\) Carry equation: \\\\(C_{i+1} = G_i + P_i C_i\\\\) Using expansion: \\\\(C_1 = G_0 + P_0 C_0\\\\) \\\\(C_2 = G_1 + P_1 G_0 + P_1 P_0 C_0\\\\) \\\\(C_3 = G_2 + P_2 G_1 + P_2 P_1 G_0 + P_2 P_1 P_0 C_0\\\\) ... \\\\(C_n\\\\) can be expressed directly using logic expressions. Fan-in restriction: The question states gates have fan-in \\u2264 2. So large AND/OR operations cannot be done in one step. They must be built as a tree of 2-input gates. A tree structure computing n inputs has depth: Depth = \\\\(\\\\log_2(n)\\\\) Therefore, Total addition time \\u221d depth of carry computation tree = \\\\(\\\\Theta(\\\\log n)\\\\)\",\n  \"options\": [],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"1.43.30\\nA. \\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n1.43.31\\nA. \\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n1.44\\n1.44.1\\nA. \\n     \\nB. \\n     \\nC. \\n     \\nD. \\n     \\n1.44.2\\ngatecse-2026-set2 algorithms time-complexity two-marks\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nTime Complexity: GATE IT 2007 | Question: 17\\nExponentiation is a heavily used operation in public key cryptography. Which of the following options is the\\ntightest upper bound on the number of multiplications required to compute \\n ?\\ngateit-2007 algorithms time-complexity normal\\nAnswer key\\u261f\",\n    \"another) into this heap. The total time required for this is\\ngatecse-2008 algorithms time-complexity normal\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nTime Complexity: GATE CSE 2008 | Question: 74\\nConsider the following C functions:\\nint f1 (int n)\\n{\\n    if(n == 0 || n == 1)\\n        return n;\\n    else\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nTo detect the fault, we should get an unexpected output. The final gate here is a NOR gate which produces output 0 if either of its input is 1 and else 1, ie, the output will be 0 for inputs (0,1), (1,0) and (1,1) and output will be 1 for (0,0). By grounding T is at 0. So, we can ignore the inputs (1,0) and (0,0) to the final NOR gate as they won't be detecting faults. Now, expected (1,1) input will become (1,0) due to grounding of T but produces same output 0 as for (1,1). Hence this also cannot detect the defect. So, to detect the defect, the input to the final gate must be (0,1) which is expected to produce a 0 but will produce a 1 due to grounding of T."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Theory of Computation\",\n  \"topic\": \"Turing Machines\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Fault detection in Turing machines through input testing\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q232\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"To detect the fault, we should get an unexpected output. The final gate here is a NOR gate which produces output 0 if either of its input is 1 and else 1, ie, the output will be 0 for inputs (0,1), (1,0) and (1,1) and output will be 1 for (0,0). By grounding T is at 0. So, we can ignore the inputs (1,0) and (0,0) to the final NOR gate as they won't be detecting faults. Now, expected (1,1) input will become (1,0) due to grounding of T but produces same output 0 as for (1,1). Hence this also cannot detect the defect. So, to detect the defect, the input to the final gate must be (0,1) which is expected to produce a 0 but will produce a 1 due to grounding of T.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"A) (0,0)\",\n    \"B) (0,1)\",\n    \"C) (1,0)\",\n    \"D) (1,1)\"\n  ],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"A. \\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n5.5.3\\nA. \\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n5.5.4\\n5.5.5\\n    program side-effect (input, output);\\n    var x, result: integer;\\n    function f (var x:integer):integer;\\n    begin\\n        x:x+1;f:=x;\\n    end\\nbegin\\n    x:=5;\\n    result:=f(x)*f(x);\\n    writeln(result);\\n    end\\ngate1998 programming normal identify-function\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nIdentify Function: GATE CSE 2017 | Set 2 | Question: 14\\nConsider the following function implemented in C:\\nvoid printxy(int x, int y) {\\n    int *ptr;\\n    x=0;\\n    ptr=&x;\",\n    \"gatecse-2026-set1 algorithms recursion numerical-answers two-marks\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nSearching (8)\\n\\u270d Practice Test: Test 1 (9Q)\\nSearching: GATE CSE 1996 | Question: 18\\nConsider the following program that attempts to locate an element  in an array \\n using binary search.\\nAssume \\n. The program is erroneous. Under what conditions does the program fail?\\nvar i,j,k: integer; x: integer;\\n    a: array; [1..N] of integer;\\nbegin i:= 1; j:= n;\\nrepeat \\n    k:(i+j) div 2;\\n    if a[k] < x then i:= k\\n    else j:= k\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
