{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\n18. ANSWER:- B\n\nExplanation: \nOption A: Again depends on the size of the\nfirst hole. It might happen the very first free\nhole is the worst possible hole. So in that\ncase size of hole decided by worst fit = size of\nhole decided by first fit. Therefore, Option A is\nFalse.\nOption B: Best fit will always the give smallest\npossible hole. So, either the hole picked will\nbe smaller than or equal to the other hole\nsizes. So, this is TRUE.\nOption C: Depends on the size of the first free\nhole. There is a very good chance that the\nsize of the first hole is small than size of the\nsize of the next hole. So, Option C is False.\nOption D: The next free hole might be the worst\npossible hole or it's size may be bigger than the\nhole picked by best fit. So, this is also False."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Algorithms\",\n  \"topic\": \"Searching\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Worst case analysis of binary search algorithm\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q444\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"18. ANSWER:- B\\n\\nExplanation: \\nOption A: Again depends on the size of the\\nfirst hole. It might happen the very first free\\nhole is the worst possible hole. So in that\\ncase size of hole decided by worst fit = size of\\nhole decided by first fit. Therefore, Option A is\\nFalse.\\nOption B: Best fit will always the give smallest\\npossible hole. So, either the hole picked will\\nbe smaller than or equal to the other hole\\nsizes. So, this is TRUE.\\nOption C: Depends on the size of the first free\\nhole. There is a very good chance that the\\nsize of the first hole is small than size of the\\nsize of the next hole. So, Option C is False.\\nOption D: The next free hole might be the worst\\npossible hole or it's size may be bigger than the\\nhole picked by best fit. So, this is also False.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"A) Again depends on the size of the first hole.\",\n    \"B) Best fit will always the give smallest possible hole.\",\n    \"C) Depends on the size of the first free hole.\",\n    \"D) The next free hole might be the worst possible hole.\"\n  ],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"problem is to \\ufb01nd the smallest index  such that \\n is  by probing the minimum number of locations in \\n.\\nThe worst case number of probes performed by an optimal algorithm is ____________.\\ngatecse-2017-set1 algorithms normal numerical-answers searching\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nSearching: GATE CSE 2025 | Set 2 | Question: 19\\nWhich of the following statements regarding Breadth First Search (BFS) and Depth First Search (DFS) on\\nan undirected simple graph \\n is/are TRUE?\\nA. A DFS tree of\",\n    \"only sometimes.\\nIV. \\n always loses to \\nWhich of the following can be logically inferred from the above statements?\\ni. \\n is likely to beat all the three other players\\nii. \\n is the absolute worst player in the set\\ngatecse-2016-set1 analytical-aptitude normal statements-follow\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nStatements Follow: GATE CSE 2016 | Set 2 | GA | Question: 08\\nAll hill-stations have a lake. Ooty has two lakes.\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\n19. ANSWER:- 155 to 155.7\n\nFor all memory accesses in a system with virtual memory we need Virtual Address to Physical Address translation and this goes through TLB.\nOn TLB hit, we get the physical address.\nOn TLB miss, we have to do page table access which always resides in physical memory (no page fault possible here).\nIn the question it is given that a 1-level page table is used. So, TLB miss will need one physical memory access to get the physical address.\nQuestion mentions page fault rate as 10% and this should default to 10 page faults every 100 memory accesses. (Since TLB hit rate is 5% and for normal programs a TLB hit and page fault cannot happen for a memory access (can happen for invalid memory accesses), it is also possible to consider page fault rate as 10% of all TLB misses. See the last part of the answer for this.)\nIn the question page transfer time is given. This is different from page fault service time which includes the page transfer time + the memory access time as once the page is filled, a new memory access is initiated."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Data Structures\",\n  \"topic\": \"Linked List\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Circularly linked list representation of a queue\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q445\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"19. ANSWER:- 155 to 155.7\\n\\nFor all memory accesses in a system with virtual memory we need Virtual Address to Physical Address translation and this goes through TLB.\\nOn TLB hit, we get the physical address.\\nOn TLB miss, we have to do page table access which always resides in physical memory (no page fault possible here).\\nIn the question it is given that a 1-level page table is used. So, TLB miss will need one physical memory access to get the physical address.\\nQuestion mentions page fault rate as 10% and this should default to 10 page faults every 100 memory accesses. (Since TLB hit rate is 5% and for normal programs a TLB hit and page fault cannot happen for a memory access (can happen for invalid memory accesses), it is also possible to consider page fault rate as 10% of all TLB misses. See the last part of the answer for this.)\\nIn the question page transfer time is given. This is different from page fault service time which includes the page transfer time + the memory access time as once the page is filled, a new memory access is initiated.\",\n  \"options\": [],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"3.10.12\\nA. rear node\\nB. front node\\nC. not possible with a single pointer\\nD. node next to front\\n3.10.13\\nA. \\n only\\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n3.10.14\\nA. \\nB. \\nC. \\nD. \\n3.10.15\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nLinked List: GATE CSE 2004 | Question: 36\\nA circularly linked list is used to represent a Queue. A single variable  is used to access the Queue. To\\nwhich node should  point such that both the operations \\n and \\n can be performed in\\nconstant time?\\ngatecse-2004 data-structures linked-list normal\\nAnswer key\\u261f\",\n    \"Answer key\\u261f\\nRuntime Environment: GATE CSE 1990 | Question: 2-v\\nMatch the pairs in the following questions:\\ngate1990 match-the-following compiler-design runtime-environment recursion\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nRuntime Environment: GATE CSE 1990 | Question: 4-v\\nState whether the following statements are TRUE or FALSE with reason:\\nThe Link-load-and-go loading scheme required less storage space than the link-and-go loading scheme.\\ngate1990 true-false compiler-design runtime-environment\\nAnswer key\\u261f\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
