{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nIn the given instruction format, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode is determined by breaking down the 32-bit instruction format. The 32-bit instruction format consists of the following fields: Opcode field, Addressing mode field, Two register operand fields, and a 16-bit scalar field. Given that we have 50 distinct types of instructions and 6 bits to represent the opcode, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible is ____."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Compiler Design\",\n  \"topic\": \"Register Allocation\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"Determining the minimum number of registers required for an optimal code generation algorithm on a load/store architecture with specific constraints.\",\n  \"question_type\": \"MCQ\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_2005_Q603\",\n  \"year\": 2005,\n  \"question\": \"In the given instruction format, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode is determined by breaking down the 32-bit instruction format. The 32-bit instruction format consists of the following fields: Opcode field, Addressing mode field, Two register operand fields, and a 16-bit scalar field. Given that we have 50 distinct types of instructions and 6 bits to represent the opcode, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible is ____.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"A) 32\",\n    \"B) 34\",\n    \"C) 36\",\n    \"D) 32\"\n  ],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"Consider the expression \\n. Let \\n be the minimum number of registers required\\nby an optimal code generation (without any register spill) algorithm for a load/store architecture, in which \\nA.  only load and store instructions can have memory operands and \\nB.  arithmetic instructions can have only register or immediate operands. \\n\\u200bThe value of \\n is _____________ .\\ngatecse-2017-set1 compiler-design register-allocation normal numerical-answers\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nRuntime Environment (22)\",\n    \"A. 0\\nB. 1\\nC. 2\\nD. 3\\n2.23.6\\n2.24\\n2.24.1\\n} \\nelse { \\n d = d * d; \\n e = e * e; \\n} \\nQ.48 Suppose the instruction set architecture of the processor has only two registers. The only allowed compiler\\noptimization is code motion, which moves statements from one place to another while preserving correctness. What\\nis the minimum number of spills to memory in the compiled code?\\ngatecse-2013 normal compiler-design register-allocation\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nRegister Allocation: GATE CSE 2017 | Set 1 | Question: 52\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are GateSter's Stage 0 Question Analyzer. Analyze raw GATE CS/IT questions and return ONLY a structured Stage 0 Blueprint JSON."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze this GATE question:\n\nThe processor has 16 general purpose registers, which means each register operand field needs log\u2082 (16) = 4 bits. There are 8 addressing modes, so the addressing mode field needs log\u2082 (8) = 3 bits. The scalar field is fixed at 16 bits. Let opcode be the number of bits allocated to the Opcode Field The bit allocation is as follows: Scalar field: 16 bits Two register operand fields: 4 + 4 = 8 bits Addressing mode field: 3 bits Total bits used by these fields: 16 + 8 + 3 = 27 bits The remaining bits for the opcode field are: 32 \u2212 27 = 5 bits Therefore, the number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode, given 5 bits, is: 2\u2075 = 32 So, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode is 32."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"subject\": \"Compiler Design\",\n  \"topic\": \"Register Allocation\",\n  \"core_concept\": \"An optimal code generation algorithm for a load/store architecture requires the minimum number of registers to execute an expression.\",\n  \"question_type\": \"NAT\",\n  \"requires_calculation\": false,\n  \"mutable_fields\": [],\n  \"immutable_fields\": [],\n  \"transformation_rules\": [],\n  \"question_id\": \"GATE_0_Q604\",\n  \"year\": 0,\n  \"question\": \"The processor has 16 general purpose registers, which means each register operand field needs log\\u2082 (16) = 4 bits. There are 8 addressing modes, so the addressing mode field needs log\\u2082 (8) = 3 bits. The scalar field is fixed at 16 bits. Let opcode be the number of bits allocated to the Opcode Field The bit allocation is as follows: Scalar field: 16 bits Two register operand fields: 4 + 4 = 8 bits Addressing mode field: 3 bits Total bits used by these fields: 16 + 8 + 3 = 27 bits The remaining bits for the opcode field are: 32 \\u2212 27 = 5 bits Therefore, the number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode, given 5 bits, is: 2\\u2075 = 32 So, the maximum number of unique opcodes possible for every addressing mode is 32.\",\n  \"options\": [],\n  \"retrieved_go_classes_notes\": [\n    \"Consider the expression \\n. Let \\n be the minimum number of registers required\\nby an optimal code generation (without any register spill) algorithm for a load/store architecture, in which \\nA.  only load and store instructions can have memory operands and \\nB.  arithmetic instructions can have only register or immediate operands. \\n\\u200bThe value of \\n is _____________ .\\ngatecse-2017-set1 compiler-design register-allocation normal numerical-answers\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nRuntime Environment (22)\",\n    \".\\na = 1 \\nb = 10\\nc = 20 \\nd = a + b\\ne = c + d \\nf = c + e \\nb = c + e \\ne = b + f \\nd = 5 + e\\nreturn d + f \\nAssuming that all operations take their operands from registers, what is the minimum number of registers needed to\\nexecute this program without spilling?\\ngatecse-2010 compiler-design register-allocation normal\\nAnswer key\\u261f\\nRegister Allocation: GATE CSE 2011 | Question: 36\\nConsider evaluating the following expression tree on a machine with load-store architecture in which\"\n  ]\n}"}]}
